; worldprops.eval — the world's properties, its areas and its doorways (W4).
;
; Everything here was hand-edited in world.map or project.ini until now. Three
; things are worth watching:
;
;   - AREA ORDER IS A RULE, not a presentation detail: they may overlap and the
;     LAST row wins. So a new area APPENDS (an exception carved out of a broad
;     region wins, which is what carving one means), and reordering is an
;     operation rather than a sort.
;   - The world's START and the GAME'S OPENING are different facts in different
;     files. The party's start is where it stands when it begins on the map;
;     the opening may be a dungeon instead.
;   - Refusals are the rules the LOADER asserts on, enforced early: a duplicate
;     id, an occupied cell, impassable ground.
logecho on
reset
worldedit on

; --- the properties, as they stand ------------------------------------------
worldprops

; --- the start refuses impassable ground (0,0 is water) ---------------------
worldprops start 0 0
worldprops start 7 6
worldprops

; --- locations: add, refuse a duplicate, move, retarget, delete -------------
worldloc
worldloc add dungeon outpost 12 6
worldloc add dungeon outpost 13 6
worldloc add dungeon crypt_gate 12 7
worldloc set outpost dungeon crypt
worldloc set outpost level crypt2
worldloc move outpost 10 10
worldloc move outpost 12 7
worldloc

; --- areas: the order rule ---------------------------------------------------
worldarea
worldarea at 6 6
worldarea add tiny 6 6 2 2 0.9
worldarea
; the newest row wins where it overlaps...
worldarea at 6 6
worldarea order tiny 0
worldarea
; ...and moving it FIRST hands the cell back to the broad region above it.
worldarea at 6 6
worldarea del tiny
worldarea

; --- and all of it is one history -------------------------------------------
undo
worldarea

; --- the rules the DIALOG sits on are the same ones ---------------------------
; W4's settings dialog edits through WorldMap::SetStart / AddArea / RemoveArea /
; MoveArea, the same calls these commands make. That is the point of moving the
; refusals into the map: there is one editor wearing two faces, so a rule proved
; here is proved for the dialog too. Three that were only in the console before:
worldarea add lowlands 0 0 4 4
worldarea add flat 3 3 0 4
; (the undo above put tiny back at row 0, so this asks for where it is)
worldarea order tiny 0
worldarea

; --- and the dialog itself opens where its button is, and nowhere else --------
; It is modal over the WORLD screen, the one state whose Update routes input to
; it; opened over a dungeon it would be a modal nothing could close.
worldmap off
worldsettings
worldmap on
worldsettings
worldsettings off
worldmap off

; --- W5: the picker's two tiers, and where a new level lands ----------------
; The toolbar dropdown is what W5 built; `levels` prints the same grouping
; through the same Project helpers, which is the part a screenshot cannot
; assert. Three facts: every level belongs to a dungeon (the orphan row is the
; control — it reads 0, so the grouping accounts for the whole manifest), a new
; level lands INSIDE the dungeon being viewed, and its stem is named after it.
; --- every project file survives being written, FIRST ------------------------
; BEFORE anything saves, so the writers are diffed against the files as they
; were hand-authored. Run AFTER a save it would be comparing the writer with
; its own output, which agrees with itself however much it drops -- and did:
; deleting the blank-line rule was invisible from here until this moved up.
catround

levels
levels new crypt
levels
